# Setup

How to set up a development machine to work on `{{ project_name }}`. Covers tool installation, authentication, and common issues.

After installing the tools in this document, `scripts/bootstrap.sh` handles the Python and project-specific parts.

## macOS prerequisites

### Homebrew

Homebrew is macOS's package manager. Install from <https://brew.sh/> if not already present:

```bash
/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"
```

Verify:

```bash
brew --version
```

### Xcode Command Line Tools

Git and several build tools ship with Xcode Command Line Tools. If `git --version` fails:

```bash
xcode-select --install
```

## Python toolchain

### uv

uv manages Python versions, virtual environments, and dependencies. Install:

```bash
brew install uv
```

Verify:

```bash
uv --version
```

uv installs Python interpreters on demand; you do not need to install Python separately. `bootstrap.sh` pins the project's Python version via `.python-version` and uv reads that.
{% if include_docker %}

## Containers

### Docker Desktop

Docker Desktop bundles the Docker engine, CLI, and a UI for managing containers. Required for `make build` and for running app images locally.

Install:

```bash
brew install --cask docker
```

Then launch Docker Desktop from Applications. The daemon must be running before any `docker` command works; you'll see a whale icon in the menu bar when it's up.

Verify:

```bash
docker --version
docker ps   # should succeed without errors
```

If you see "Cannot connect to the Docker daemon", Docker Desktop isn't running. Launch it from Applications.

Recommended Docker Desktop settings (Preferences → Resources):

- 4 GB RAM minimum, 8 GB if you can spare it
- 2 CPUs minimum
- Disk image size 60 GB+ (images and build cache add up)

### Docker Hub sign-in

Docker Desktop prompts for a Docker Hub login on first launch. You can skip this for local builds; sign-in is required only for pushing public images to Docker Hub. This project pushes to Google Artifact Registry instead.
{% endif %}
{% if use_gcp %}

## GCP

### gcloud CLI

The gcloud CLI talks to Google Cloud Platform. Required for `make deploy` (pushes container images to Artifact Registry, deploys to Cloud Run) and for any direct GCP work.

Install:

```bash
brew install --cask google-cloud-sdk
```

After install, restart your shell or run `source ~/.zshrc` (or `~/.bash_profile`).

Verify:

```bash
gcloud --version
```

### Authentication

GCP requires two kinds of authentication: your user account (for `gcloud` commands you run directly) and application-default credentials (ADC, used by Google client libraries inside Python code).

```bash
gcloud auth login                       # user account
gcloud auth application-default login   # ADC for libraries
gcloud config set project {{ gcp_project_dev }}
```

Verify:

```bash
gcloud auth list                # should show your account with (active)
gcloud config list              # should show project = {{ gcp_project_dev }}
gcloud auth application-default print-access-token   # should print a token
```

ADC expires periodically. If `make deploy` or local Python code fails with auth errors, re-run `gcloud auth application-default login`.
{% endif %}
{% if include_terraform %}

## Infrastructure

### Terraform

Terraform manages cloud resources via declarative config files. Required for `make tf-plan` and `make tf-apply`.

```bash
brew install terraform
```

Verify:

```bash
terraform --version
```

This project requires Terraform 1.10 or newer. If `brew install terraform` gives you an older version, run `brew upgrade terraform`.

### tflint (optional)

A linter for Terraform configurations. Not required, but useful for catching issues before `terraform plan`:

```bash
brew install tflint
```
{% endif %}

## Project tooling

### pre-commit

pre-commit runs linters and formatters on staged files before each commit. `bootstrap.sh` installs it into the project's hooks directory. To make the `pre-commit` command available globally (for running checks manually or working in other repos):

```bash
brew install pre-commit
```

### copier (template updates)

Required only when you want to pull the latest template changes into the project via `copier update --trust`:

```bash
brew install copier
```

## AI tooling

### Claude Code

Claude Code is the AI coding assistant used in this project. Install the VS Code extension from the Extensions panel (search "Claude Code"), or the CLI:

```bash
npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code
```

Verify:

```bash
claude --version
```

## First-run verification

After installing everything you need, from the project root:

```bash
./scripts/bootstrap.sh
```

bootstrap.sh:

1. Installs/updates uv if missing
2. Installs Python at the version specified in `.python-version`
3. Syncs project dependencies via `uv sync`
4. Installs pre-commit hooks
{% if use_gcp -%}
5. Verifies gcloud auth and default project
{% endif -%}
6. Warns about any missing optional tools (Docker, gcloud, Terraform)

Then verify the project itself runs:

```bash
make ci   # lint + typecheck + test
```

If `make ci` passes, your machine is set up correctly.

## Troubleshooting

### `uv: command not found` after install

Restart your shell, or run `source ~/.zshrc` (or your shell's rc file). If still missing, check that `/opt/homebrew/bin` (Apple Silicon) or `/usr/local/bin` (Intel) is in your `PATH`.
{% if include_docker %}

### `docker: Cannot connect to the Docker daemon`

Docker Desktop isn't running. Launch it from Applications and wait for the whale icon to appear in the menu bar with "Docker Desktop is running".

### `make build` fails with "no space left on device"

Docker's disk allocation is full. In Docker Desktop: Preferences → Resources, increase disk image size. Then clear unused images:

```bash
docker system prune -a
```
{% endif %}

### `Permission denied (publickey)` from GitHub

Your SSH key isn't registered with GitHub. See the template repo's README "GitHub authentication" section for setup.
{% if use_gcp %}

### `Reauthentication required` from gcloud

Application Default Credentials expired:

```bash
gcloud auth application-default login
```

### `User does not have permission` from gcloud

Your user account isn't granted the role you need in the GCP project. For deploy work, the typical roles are `roles/run.developer`, `roles/artifactregistry.writer`, and `roles/iam.serviceAccountUser`. These can be granted in the GCP console under IAM or via `gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding`.
{% endif %}
{% if include_terraform %}

### `Error acquiring the state lock` from terraform

Another `terraform apply` is running, or a previous run was interrupted and the lock wasn't released. If you're sure no one else is running terraform, force-unlock:

```bash
terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID>
```

The `LOCK_ID` is printed in the error message.

### `Error: Failed to get existing workspaces: querying Cloud Storage failed`

The state bucket doesn't exist yet, or your gcloud credentials don't have access. For a brand-new project, run `./scripts/tf-bootstrap.sh` once to create the state buckets. After that, ensure ADC is set up.
{% endif %}

### `bootstrap.sh` warns about a missing tool

Warnings about missing optional tools are non-fatal. Each tool is needed only for the part of the project that uses it ({% if include_docker %}Docker for container builds, {% endif %}{% if use_gcp %}gcloud for deploys{% if include_terraform %}, Terraform for infrastructure{% endif %}{% endif %}). Install the relevant tool from the section above, then re-run `./scripts/bootstrap.sh`.

### Pre-commit hook fails on a file you didn't change

The hook auto-formatted the file. Stage the formatted changes and commit again:

```bash
git add <file>
git commit
```

### `make new-app` rejects a name

App names must be `kebab-case` (lowercase letters, digits, hyphens; starts with a letter; no underscores). The script converts the kebab name to `snake_case` for the Python package name internally. Example: `make new-app NAME=invoice-sync` creates `apps/invoice-sync/` with Python package `invoice_sync`.

### Tests fail with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '<app>'`

Run `uv sync` to make sure the workspace dependencies are installed. If that doesn't help, the package may not be declared as a workspace member; check that the app's directory is listed in the root `pyproject.toml`'s `[tool.uv.workspace]` block.
